# .pii-allow — lines the PII scan may skip in THIS repo.
# One regex per line. Keep each one narrow: an allow entry is a permanent
# exception, and a broad pattern quietly disables the control it lives under.
#
# `.pii-terms` (gitignored, machine-global) lists the private vocabulary. This
# file records the places where a term is deliberately, publicly correct.

# The copyright holder. AGPL requires the licensor be named, so the entity name
# is intentionally public here — that is the opposite of a leak.
Copyright .*Brightside Data LLC
Brightside Data LLC.*(All rights reserved|AGPL|licen[sc]e)
^\s*\*?\s*Bellows, by JGOBuilds

# Vendor product names inside the DETECTION lists themselves. The employer guard
# and the commit-msg gate work by matching tool names, so the names have to be
# in the source to be caught anywhere else. Narrow to the regex/tuple lines.
snowflake\|bigquery\|redshift\|databricks\|synapse\|quantexa
^\s*"quantexa",$

# A public company mapped to its public careers URL. This is data ABOUT a
# job target, not about the user, and the ATS lookup cannot be tested without it.
ats_url_for\("The Hartford"
